"Beolach" <beolach@gmail.com> writes:

>  But there's one thing I'm still a bit unclear on, and I'm now at a
> point in my game that I'd feel a lot better knowing.  Converting altars
> is done by #offer on a non-aligned altar, and earlier I (chaotic Elven
> Wizard) converted a neutral altar by sacing a white unicorn's
> corpse.

"non-coaligned" or "cross-aligned" would be a better term than
"non-aligned" or "unaligned", which are reserved to refer to altars of
Moloch.

> What I'm unclear on is does the corpse being sacrificed have to be a
> unicorn?  There seems to be a lot of emphasis on sacing unicorns, but
> I'm not sure if that's only because they're dangerous if you sacrifice
> them wrong, or if they're the only corpses that qualify for converting
> the altar.  From source diving I'm pretty sure any sacrifice works, but
> if somebody can tell me that outright it'll easy my mind.

Do not sacrifice your own race unless you're chaotic.  Do not
sacrifice unicorns unless you know what you're doing (clarification at
the end of this post).  Apart from that, any sacrifice works for
conversion and has exactly the same chance of converting the altar.

The boring details about unicorns: white unicorns are lawful, gray are
neutral, and black are chaotic.  Sacrificing a unicorn on an altar of
its own alignment infuriates the deity of that alignment.  Sacrificing
a unicorn of your own alignment on an altar of a different alignment
converts *you*, which is a Bad Idea, especially if you haven't done
your quest yet.

Sacrificing a unicorn of a different alignment on a coaligned altar is
an exceptionally good sacrifice.  All other cases (you, altar and
unicorn are of three different alignments) are simply a normal attempt
to convert the altar.  Some helpful charts showing the effects of
sacrificing unicorns can be found at
http://www.nethack.de/spoiler/32unioffer.txt
